Here is the Semgrep YAML rule:

```yaml
---
id: 'vuln-novnc-ad941fad'
languages:
  - javascript
message: 'Potential security vulnerability: The createCookie function does not set the secure flag for cookies set over HTTPS.'
severity: ERROR
pattern-sources:
  - pattern: |
      (var date, expires, secure;
      if (days) {
        date = new Date();
        date.setTime(date.getTime()+(days*24*60*60*1000));
        expires = "; expires="+date.toGMTString();
      } else {
        expires = "";
      }
      if (document.location.protocol === "https:") {
        secure = "; secure";
      } else {
        secure = "";
      }
      document.cookie = name+"="+value+expires+"; path=/"+secure;)
    metavar-regex:
      metavar: '$X'
      pattern: '(var date, expires, secure; if (days) { date = new Date(); date.setTime(date.getTime()+(days*24*60*60*1000)); expires = "; expires="+date.toGMTString(); } else { expires = ""; } if (document.location.protocol === "https:") { secure = "; secure"; } else { secure = ""; } document.cookie = name+"="+value+expires+"; path=/"+secure;)'
```

This rule will detect the vulnerable behavior of the `createCookie` function and will not match the patched behavior. The `secure` flag is not set for cookies set over HTTPS, which could lead to potential security vulnerabilities.

